WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.860
Output encoding is the practice of preparing data safely for the specific context where it will be rendered, transmitted, or consumed.

2
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.760
The key phrase is specific context.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:22.140
A value that is safe as plain text may not be safe inside an HTML attribute, a JavaScript string, a URL, a CSS value, a CSV cell, or a log viewer.

4
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:31.040
Encoding preserves the meaning of the value as data so it is not accidentally interpreted as markup, script, syntax, or active content.

5
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:37.720
Encoding is related to validation, sanitization, and escaping, but those terms are not interchangeable.

6
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.580
Validation decides whether input is acceptable for the application.

7
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:48.400
Sanitization attempts to remove or transform unsafe parts of content, usually when some rich content must be allowed.

8
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.980
Escaping is often the mechanical act of replacing special characters with safe representations.

9
00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:03.480
Output encoding is broader: it means choosing the correct transformation for the destination context, at the moment data leaves the application boundary.

10
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.400
Trusted and untrusted data can both become dangerous in the wrong context.

11
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:25.220
A display name, product title, support note, CSV export field, markdown comment, or error message may start as ordinary data, then become unsafe when inserted into a browser page, script block, spreadsheet, report, or log console.

12
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:32.280
Output encoding is a core defense against injection into rendered output because it keeps data from becoming executable code or markup.

13
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.860
The goal is not to make all text look strange.

14
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.120
The goal is to preserve data meaning exactly where the data is used.
